{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"- A generator can still produce hallucinations even when it retrieves correct facts because the model must synthesize and \\\"fill in\\\" gaps using learned patterns. The context says LLMs largely mimic training data and often only see final outputs (not intermediate thought processes). When the model reconstructs an answer it can combine retrieved facts incorrectly or invent plausible links where reasoning traces are missing.\\n\\n- Main causes given in the context:\\n  - Training on AI-generated web data (AI inbreeding / model collapse). If models are trained on generations of AI outputs, mistakes and shortcuts can propagate and limit improvement, increasing the chance of producing confident but incorrect content.\\n  - Web data is mostly final outputs; the intermediate reasoning, debugging, and thought-process data are largely absent. That missing data forces the model to guess internal steps, which can produce hallucinations despite correct retrieval of surface facts.\\n  - LLMs are black-box mimickers. The model may appear to reason (per experiments noted in the context) but cannot be proven to do so; its internal synthesis can nonetheless produce errors.\\n\\nNot covered in the context: precise internal mechanisms of hallucination (e.g., token-level scoring, beam search dynamics, probability thresholds), quantitative rates, or mitigation techniques.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"A generator can hallucinate even when retrieval is correct by ignoring the retrieved context and producing an answer from its own assumptions or from unrelated parts of the context. This happens because the retriever may fetch the right documents, but the generator can still misread them, over-prioritize certain text, or add unsupported facts.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"How can a generator hallucinate despite correct retrieval, and what causes it?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"A\\nLet's say chat GPT model\\